<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ly dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1009 CE – 1225 CE · capital Thang Long</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty that founded Thang Long, today's Hanoi, built Vietnam's first lasting state institutions and turned back a Song invasion in 1077.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1009 the court official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Ly Cong Uan</a>, raised in a Buddhist monastery, was chosen to succeed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-le-dynasty">Early Le dynasty</a> and reigned as Ly Thai To. In 1010 he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lu</a> to the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Dai La</a>, renaming it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a>, 'rising dragon'. In 1054 the state was renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tran-dynasty">Dai Viet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ly rulers combined Buddhist patronage with Confucian government. They built the One Pillar Pagoda, founded the Temple of Literature in 1070 and held the first civil service examination in 1075. Dikes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-asia">Red River</a> and a law code strengthened the state. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thuong-kiet">Ly Thuong Kiet</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song China</a> in 1075 and defeated a Song counter-invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nhu-nguyet-river">Nhu Nguyet</a> river in 1077.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the late 12th century weak rulers let regional clans gain power, and the Tran family came to dominate the court. In 1225 the young empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-chieu-hoang">Ly Chieu Hoang</a> was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-thai-tong">Tran Canh</a> and abdicated in his favour, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tran-dynasty">Tran dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ly-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ly dynasty" width="320" height="345"><figcaption>Image: Night Lantern · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam_(1009-1225).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1009 CE – 1225 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thang Long</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ly Thai To, Ly Thanh Tong, Ly Nhan Tong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese, Classical Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Ly dynasty, Dai Viet, Đại Việt, Nhà Lý</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1117&amp;focus=ly-dynasty" aria-label="Ly dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7526 1400 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ly dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7846 1656L7856 1684M7846 1656L7901 1174M7846 1656L7846 1656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7856 1684h0M7901 1174h0M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1117&amp;focus=ly-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
